WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:06.000
Missing sisters, catastrophic explosions,&nbsp;
and year-long pregnancies. For a show about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.407
physicists, The Big Bang Theory&nbsp;
has a lot of missing pieces.

00:00:10.407 --> 00:00:12.720
Some of the most recognizable hangout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:18.000
spots in television history are Central Perk's&nbsp;
orange couch on Friends, the table at MacLaren's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.680
Pub on How I Met Your Mother, and the sofa in&nbsp;
Leonard and Sheldon's apartment on The Big Bang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:27.540
Theory. Much like how the orange couch in Central&nbsp;
Perk is seemingly reserved for the six Friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:32.220
and the "Main Five" of How I Met Your Mother&nbsp;
have unofficial dibs on the central table at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:37.800
MacLaren's Pub, Sheldon has claimed a particular&nbsp;
spot on his apartment's sofa as eternally his.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:41.940
Sheldon is shown to have little to no&nbsp;
patience for anyone who sits in his spot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.540
and has openly admitted that his feelings&nbsp;
for the spot are even stronger than his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:50.940
love for his mother. His affection towards this&nbsp;
particular spot isn't specific to the sofa itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:54.720
as shown in the flashback episode&nbsp;
"The Staircase Implementation." Here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.380
we see that the only furniture in the living&nbsp;
room was a pair of lawn chairs, one of which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:03.540
was Sheldon's kept in almost the exact same area&nbsp;
his seat on the couch would occupy down the line.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:08.220
However, there are several instances throughout&nbsp;
the show when the other characters sit on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:13.200
spot in Sheldon's presence, and he doesn't seem to&nbsp;
mind or even notice it. Given how finicky Sheldon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.640
is about the spot, it seems unlikely that he'd&nbsp;
be okay with anyone else seated there, with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:22.958
exception of his beloved grandmother, which makes&nbsp;
this a possible oversight by the cast and crew.

00:01:22.958 --> 00:01:25.020
Kayley Cuoco's character Penny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.580
has occasionally shared tidbits about her&nbsp;
quirky family from Omaha, Nebraska. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.960
the exception of her father, Wyatt, the rest of&nbsp;
Penny's family went most of the length of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.980
show without making an appearance on screen.&nbsp;
Aside from her father, Penny has mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:42.677
she has a brother and a sister, both of whom&nbsp;
she's comedically described as wild cards.

00:01:42.677 --> 00:01:44.063
"What's he on trial for?"

00:01:44.063 --> 00:01:49.020
"Oh, just a big misunderstanding. Ya know, you'd&nbsp;
actually like my brother. He's kind of a chemist."

00:01:51.689 --> 00:01:56.400
While Penny's brother Randall and their mother&nbsp;
Susan finally appeared for Penny and Leonard's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.900
second wedding ceremony in the first episode of&nbsp;
Season 10, "The Conjugal Conjecture," her sister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:05.220
was nowhere to be seen. Given that there was no&nbsp;
mention of a sister in the episode, it's quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:09.180
likely that the writers either overlooked the&nbsp;
fact that Penny had a sister, or the budget had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.500
already been exhausted on the episode's ensemble&nbsp;
cast that was appearing for the wedding ceremony.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:16.920
Bernadette's pregnancy was revealed in Season 9,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:22.860
Episode 15, "The Valentino Submergence." In the&nbsp;
episode, Howard and Bernadette save an unconscious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:27.780
rabbit found in their new hot tub and name it&nbsp;
"Valentino Wolowitz Rabbit," after the holiday.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.220
We see the couple's parental instincts emerge as&nbsp;
the two nurture the rabbit back to health — that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:37.260
is until Howard gets bitten by Valentino and&nbsp;
fears that he may have been infected with rabies.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.420
After Howard leaves to get himself tested,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:42.801
Bernadette reveals some startling&nbsp;
news to him and the audience.

00:02:43.087 --> 00:02:45.180
"We'll find another time&nbsp;
to tell him I'm pregnant."

00:02:47.468 --> 00:02:51.780
Given that the episode takes place on Valentine's&nbsp;
Day and that it typically takes two to three weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:56.460
after conception for a pregnancy test to show&nbsp;
a positive result, it's safe to say that Howard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:01.080
and Bernadette must have conceived sometime in&nbsp;
January. Their child, Halley, was finally born&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:05.700
in Season 10, Episode 11, which takes place&nbsp;
in December. This means that Bernadette's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:10.063
pregnancy lasted 11 months, two months longer&nbsp;
than the typical duration of a pregnancy.

00:03:11.007 --> 00:03:12.780
The broken elevator in Sheldon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:16.860
Leonard, and Penny's building served as a&nbsp;
running gag for the entirety of the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:21.360
run. This long standing aspect of the story&nbsp;
allows writers to make use of the building's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.280
staircases to lengthen character exchanges,&nbsp;
both to provide exposition or to simply add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:31.200
some awkward banter between any characters&nbsp;
who happened to climb the stairs to 4A and 4B.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:35.160
It was revealed in the Season 3 episode&nbsp;
"The Staircase Implementation" that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:39.360
elevator broke as a result of Leonard's&nbsp;
failed experiment in 2003. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:43.320
Leonard tells Howard in the Season 1&nbsp;
episode "The Nerdvana Annihilation"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:47.400
that the elevator has been broken for&nbsp;
two years. As the show started in 2007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:52.560
this would mean the elevator broke in 2005.&nbsp;
What's more, Howard witnessed the elevator break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.180
in "The Staircase Implementation", so there's no&nbsp;
reason Leonard would need to explain this to him.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:02.580
It's strange enough that the elevator took until&nbsp;
the series finale, a full 16 years from when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:07.200
initially broke, to finally get fixed. However,&nbsp;
what's more puzzling is the fact that Sheldon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:11.940
never seemed to be irritated by the elevator's&nbsp;
malfunction, as he often berated the landlord&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:16.429
and building association for issues that were&nbsp;
far more trivial than the broken elevator.

00:04:17.287 --> 00:04:19.200
Though Raj's proficiency in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.880
native language Hindi was never in doubt, in the&nbsp;
Season 10 episode "The Cognition Regeneration,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:28.200
Raj admits something that entirely contradicts&nbsp;
his character's background. In the episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.920
Sheldon decides to challenge himself&nbsp;
cognitively by performing difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:35.340
mental tasks after losing at a&nbsp;
multiplayer game with the gang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:39.000
and begins to worry that he's no&nbsp;
longer the wunderkind he once was.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:43.980
Raj suggests Sheldon learn Hindi, and&nbsp;
Sheldon surprises him by responding in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:48.060
Hindi that he had learned the language&nbsp;
when he was eight years old. Raj admits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:51.540
that he himself never learned Hindi&nbsp;
nor does he understand the language.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:55.800
Raj mentioned several times that he had&nbsp;
been born and raised by Hindi-speaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:58.980
parents in New Delhi, India, a&nbsp;
country he complains about when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:02.534
faced with the possibility of getting&nbsp;
deported in "The Pirate Solution."

00:05:02.534 --> 00:05:07.440
"I don't want to go back to India. It's&nbsp;
hot, and loud, and there's so many people."

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:12.540
Raj has also occasionally spoken a few words&nbsp;
in Hindi. He sings a lullaby to himself in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:17.100
Hindi when cornered by the chatty Penny in "The&nbsp;
Big Bran Hypothesis," and responds "whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:21.300
floats your boat" in Hindi to Sheldon's&nbsp;
attempt at learning Finnish in "The Psychic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.660
Vortex." Though Raj isn't too fond&nbsp;
of his country's food and culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:29.220
he's shown a fascination with Bollywood movies,&nbsp;
most notably when he has a heated debate with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:33.598
Sheldon about two prominent Bollywood&nbsp;
actresses in "The Bad Fish Paradigm."

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:38.040
There are two facts about Howard Wolowitz&nbsp;
that consistently make him the butt of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:41.820
jokes on "The Big Bang Theory": his deeply&nbsp;
unhealthy relationship with his mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:46.140
and the fact that he's the only one&nbsp;
in the gang without a Ph.D. Howard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:49.620
as it turns out, only has a&nbsp;
Master's from MIT. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:53.700
these two aspects of the character happen to&nbsp;
contradict each other when given some thought.

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:58.740
Howard's relationship with his possessive and&nbsp;
controlling mother Debbie Wolowitz was often a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:02.880
source of running gags throughout the show.&nbsp;
Shown to be a typical "momma's boy," Howard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:07.500
had never moved out of his mother's house in&nbsp;
Pasadena, nor could he muster up the courage to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:11.760
do so when asked by his wife Bernadette in the&nbsp;
season 6 premiere, "The Date Night Variable."

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:16.020
However, the Massachusetts Institute&nbsp;
of Technology is a university located&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:20.580
on the other side of the country, and it would&nbsp;
take at least 5 years for Howard to complete a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:25.309
Masters there. It's unimaginable to think that&nbsp;
Howard could've survived on his own that long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.309 --> 00:06:28.795
or that Debbie would allow him to&nbsp;
move to Boston in the first place.

